# Agricultural Income Tax Amendment Bill
Tamil title: விவசாய வருமான வரி - திருத்தச் சட்ட முன்வடிவு

Dated 17 July 1967

The Honourable C. N. Annadurai:

Mr Speaker, agricultural income tax is a scheme accepted by this House. It is not merely an accepted scheme; it is one already in operation. What is now being done is to amend it properly, with the two objectives of establishing social justice through it and bringing the government a little additional revenue.

Several amendments have been proposed. In response to members of the House belonging to various parties pointing out provisions regarded as particularly severe, it has been decided to refer the Bill to a Select Committee. The Leader of the Opposition said that although it is not customary for a person who is to become a member of the Select Committee to speak at this stage, there are occasions on which one may do so.

There is no great fault in speaking. One might even say that it gives us an opportunity to learn what they intend to say in the Select Committee. But from the fact that they propose to speak still more extensively in the Select Committee about everything they have said now, they do not appear to regard it as a *porukku* committee, a committee that selects. I suspect they want it to be a *norukku* committee, a committee that smashes the amendment to pieces. We must therefore examine all these matters in the Select Committee with an awareness that we accept agricultural income tax in principle.

The Leader of the Opposition said that because we have granted some concession to dry land, we are using that as a pretext to extract more through this measure. Members have insisted that such concessions should be extended not only to dry land but also to wet land. The government has assured the House that this will be considered. Further concessions suited to landowners and reasonable in extent will therefore be provided. There is no need to imagine that, because tax relief was granted to dry land, the government seeks greater revenue through some other route.

The Leader of the Opposition said that this measure would yield twenty-five crore rupees. That was when I said it might even be leased out. If they give me ten crore, collect twenty-five crore, and keep the remaining fifteen crore themselves, the law will not permit it. We do not expect revenue on that scale. We expect only a few lakh rupees in additional income; we are not thinking in terms of crores. It will not yield that much.

If the land-ceiling law is administered properly, agricultural income tax will not apply to a very large number of taxpayers. Once the land-ceiling law is implemented properly, there is no need to fear this tax. If people do fear it, that reveals that they have evaded the land-ceiling law. This measure is therefore a law that closes the loophole in the land-ceiling law. The larger that loophole has grown, the larger the plug required to close it. If there is no loophole, this law itself becomes ineffectual. If there is no loophole, will agricultural income tax be obtained? It will not.

They have accepted one point, and I am pleased. The Leader of the Opposition said concessions need not be shown to cash crops; they should be shown to food crops. I welcome that statement with great gratitude. Agricultural income is generally greater from cash crops than from food crops. This government does not hold, nor does any government hold, that cash crops must be prohibited altogether. When greater income is earned from them, giving a portion to the government is one of the democratic duties of those earning it.

Under the method now proposed, we expect greater revenue according to the income yielded by each crop. For example, it was said that even cultivating one-fifth of an acre in grapes produces substantial income. When all of you sit and deliberate in the Select Committee, agricultural statistics will be presented. If the proposal says that one-fifth yields a certain amount and a change is required, it may be altered to one-fourth or one-third.

As member Sankarayya said, the fundamental purpose is to obtain more revenue than at present. I very much hope the members of the Select Committee will act with that aim.

I thank everyone for not denying either the necessity of agricultural income tax or the fact that it is already in operation.

Mr R. Ponnappa Nadar:

On a point of clarification, sir. Under the Travancore-Cochin Agricultural Income Tax law, the ceiling has been fixed at three thousand rupees. Under this Bill, it is proposed to raise it to four thousand rupees. The Travancore-Cochin law remains in force in Kanyakumari district. Is there an intention to amend that law, or is it proposed to extend this law to that area?

The Honourable C. N. Annadurai:

Mr Speaker, generally speaking, ever since the Kanyakumari region joined Tamil Nadu, its people have displayed a clever attitude. They ask us to give them whichever laws in force here are advantageous to them, while asking that whichever laws already in force there are advantageous to them should remain exactly as they are. I have observed this cleverness for ten years.

The course the government will follow is this: whichever laws enable revenue to be increased will be extended to Kanyakumari.

Mr T. S. Adhimoolam:

On a point of order, sir. In this Select Committee of twenty-four members, only one person has been appointed on behalf of the Swatantra Party. This is an act of ingratitude...

The Honourable Speaker:

That is not a point of order.

The Honourable C. N. Annadurai:

Mr Speaker, the honourable Adhimoolam regrets that only one member has been appointed on behalf of the Swatantra Party. Even half of us are people who support you. Our very presence here is for your sake.

---NOTES---
The source is a Legislative Assembly debate with interventions by R. Ponnappa Nadar and T. S. Adhimoolam and a ruling from the Speaker. Speaker changes are preserved.

“பொறுக்குக் கமிட்டி” is the period Tamil rendering used for a Select Committee. Anna turns it into a pun with “நொறுக்குக் கமிட்டி,” literally a committee that crushes or smashes. Both the institutional meaning and wordplay are retained.

“புன்செய்” and “நன்செய்” are rendered as dry land and wet land respectively, referring to rain-fed and irrigated agricultural categories.

The statement about leasing collection for ten crore against an expected twenty-five crore is rhetorical. The monetary figures and crop fractions are translated exactly as stated.

Political, fiscal, agricultural, and historical assertions are translated in each speaker's parliamentary voice and have not been independently verified.
